SLIDES_PART1 = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'We the Travellers', 'subtitle': 'Human Fascination with Travel from Ancient Times to Spacecraft', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">Since the dawn of human history, people have loved to travel, explore new lands, meet distant communities, and discover the unknown. Thousands of years ago, the only way to travel was by walking on foot. Gradually, humans tamed animals like bullocks, horses, camels, and yaks to pull carts and carry heavy loads across deserts, grasslands, and mountain trails.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">Ancient Travel (0 to 15 km/h)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Walking at 3–5 km/h, riding bullock carts at 4 km/h, or sailing timber boats propelled by wind and oars across rivers and coastal bays.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Modern Era (80 to 28,000 km/h)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">High-speed electric trains travelling at 160 km/h, commercial aeroplanes at 850 km/h, and spacecraft orbiting at 28,000 km/h.</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">As travel speeds and distances increased, humans needed larger numbers to measure travel distances, count passengers, calculate vehicle fuel, and map trade routes across vast continents.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Welcome young mathematicians! Since the earliest days of human civilization, people have had an adventurous desire to travel, explore new lands, and trade with distant communities. In ancient times, the only way to travel was by walking on foot at a gentle pace of three to five kilometres per hour. Later, humans learned to domesticate animals like bullocks, horses, and camels, pulling wooden carts at four to five kilometres per hour, and building timber boats to cross rivers. But modern technology changed everything! Today, high-speed electric trains like the Vande Bharat glide across our country at one hundred sixty kilometres per hour. Commercial aeroplanes fly through the clouds at eight hundred fifty kilometres per hour, and spacecraft soar into orbit at a breathtaking speed of twenty-eight thousand kilometres per hour! When speeds and travel distances expanded from just ten kilometres to thousands and millions of kilometres, humans needed much larger numbers to count travellers, measure distances, calculate vehicle fuel, and chart transport networks. This is why we study numbers beyond thousands, entering the wondrous realm of ten thousand. Look at the timeline simulator on your screen, test the speeds of different eras, and observe how our world grew closer through the power of mathematics!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If an ancient traveller walked at an average speed of 4 km every hour, how far could they travel in 6 hours of steady walking?', 'options': ['10 km', '24 km', '40 km', '64 km'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Distance = Speed × Time. At 4 km per hour, walking for 6 hours covers 4 × 6 = 24 km.', 'study_insight': "In ancient times, a 24 km journey was considered a full day's travel between neighbouring villages."}, {'question': 'Which modern mode of transport travels at a speed of approximately 28,000 km per hour?', 'options': ['A bullock cart', 'A city bicycle', 'A spacecraft orbiting the Earth', 'A passenger bus'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'A spacecraft such as the International Space Station orbits Earth at approximately 28,000 km per hour, circling the entire globe every 90 minutes.', 'study_insight': 'At 28,000 km/h, a spacecraft travels about 7.7 kilometres in a single second!'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Ten Thousand', 'subtitle': 'Discovering 5-Digit Numbers: Moving from 9,000 to 10,000', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">In Grade 4, you mastered 4-digit numbers up to 9,999. Now let us discover what happens when we add thousands successively. Imagine counting passenger seats on long-distance trains:</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3 font-mono text-xs text-center space-y-1">\n                <span class="text-slate-400">1,000 → 2,000 → 3,000 → 4,000 → 5,000 → 6,000 → 7,000 → 8,000 → 9,000</span><br>\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold">9,000 + 1,000 = 10,000 (Ten Thousand)</span>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Just as 10 Hundreds bundle together to make <strong>1 Thousand</strong> (10 × 100 = 1,000), bundling 10 Thousands together gives us <strong>1 Ten Thousand</strong> (10 × 1,000 = 10,000).</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 my-3 text-xs text-emerald-200">\n                <strong>Key Milestone:</strong> 10,000 is the <em>smallest 5-digit number</em>. It is written as 1 followed by four zeroes (1, 0, 0, 0, 0). The largest 4-digit number is 9,999, and adding 1 to 9,999 gives exactly 10,000!\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Let us understand how large numbers are born from smaller ones by looking at the base-ten building blocks. In primary school, you learned that ten single unit cubes bundle together to make one ten rod. Ten of these rods make one flat hundred square, and ten hundred squares stack together to form one solid thousand cube. Now, what happens when we continue this journey in thousands? Suppose you have nine thousand cubes, which is nine thousand. If you add one more thousand cube, you have ten thousand cubes. In our decimal number system, we never write ten digits in a single place value column. Instead, all ten thousand cubes bundle together to form a brand new, massive unit called Ten Thousand! We write ten thousand as one followed by four zeros: one, zero, zero, zero, zero. It is the smallest five-digit number in the world. Just one number before it is nine thousand nine hundred ninety-nine, which is the largest four-digit number. When we add one to nine thousand nine hundred ninety-nine, we arrive right at ten thousand! Use the interactive block counter to bundle thousands one by one and witness the birth of ten thousand!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What number do you get when you add 1 to the largest 4-digit number, 9,999?', 'options': ['9,990', '10,000 (Ten Thousand)', '100,000 (One Lakh)', '99,999'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Adding 1 to 9,999 gives 10,000, which is the smallest 5-digit number.', 'study_insight': '9,999 + 1 = 10,000. In the place value system, adding 1 causes a cascade of regrouping across all 4 columns into the Ten Thousand column.'}, {'question': 'How many thousands are bundled together to form Ten Thousand (10,000)?', 'options': ['5 Thousands', '10 Thousands', '100 Thousands', '1,000 Thousands'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '10 Thousands = 1 Ten Thousand (10 × 1,000 = 10,000). The base-10 system always bundles ten of any unit to make one of the next higher unit.', 'study_insight': '10 Ones = 1 Ten; 10 Tens = 1 Hundred; 10 Hundreds = 1 Thousand; 10 Thousands = 1 Ten Thousand.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Place Value', 'subtitle': 'The Indian Place Value Chart: TTh, Th, H, T, and O', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">To read and write large numbers systematically, we use the <strong>Indian Place Value System</strong>. With 5-digit numbers, we add a brand-new column to the left of Thousands: <strong>Ten Thousands (TTh)</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="overflow-x-auto my-3">\n                <table class="w-full text-center text-xs border border-slate-800 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">\n                    <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-amber-400 font-bold">\n                        <tr>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">TTh (Ten Thousands)</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">Th (Thousands)</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">H (Hundreds)</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">T (Tens)</th>\n                            <th class="p-2 border border-slate-800">O (Ones)</th>\n                        </tr>\n                    </thead>\n                    <tbody class="bg-slate-950/70 text-slate-200">\n                        <tr>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-bold text-sky-400">3</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-bold text-sky-400">5</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-bold text-emerald-400">4</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-bold text-amber-400">2</td>\n                            <td class="p-2 border border-slate-800 font-bold text-rose-400">7</td>\n                        </tr>\n                    </tbody>\n                </table>\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">In the number <strong>35,427</strong>:</p>\n            <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 list-disc list-inside space-y-1 my-2">\n                <li>Digit <strong>3</strong> is in Ten Thousands place → Value = 3 × 10,000 = 30,000</li>\n                <li>Digit <strong>5</strong> is in Thousands place → Value = 5 × 1,000 = 5,000</li>\n                <li>Digit <strong>4</strong> is in Hundreds place → Value = 4 × 100 = 400</li>\n                <li>Digit <strong>2</strong> is in Tens place → Value = 2 × 10 = 20</li>\n                <li>Digit <strong>7</strong> is in Ones place → Value = 7 × 1 = 7</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Expanded Form: <strong>30,000 + 5,000 + 400 + 20 + 7 = 35,427</strong>.</p>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now let us examine the Indian Place Value Chart for five-digit numbers. Every five-digit number has five distinct columns, read from right to left: Ones, Tens, Hundreds, Thousands, and our new column, Ten Thousands, abbreviated as T, T, h. Each step to the left increases the value of a digit by ten times! For instance, a digit in the Ones column represents single units. In the Tens column, it is worth ten times as much. In the Hundreds column, it is worth a hundred times. In the Thousands column, a thousand times. And in the Ten Thousands column, that same digit is worth ten thousand times its face value! Consider the number thirty-four thousand two hundred eighty-five. The digit three sits in the Ten Thousands place, giving it a value of thirty thousand. The digit four is in the Thousands place, representing four thousand. The digit two is in the Hundreds place, worth two hundred. The digit eight in Tens is eighty, and five in Ones is five. In expanded form, we write it as thirty thousand, plus four thousand, plus two hundred, plus eighty, plus five. Click on each spike of the interactive abacus on your screen to add beads and watch the expanded form build up dynamically!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the place value of the digit 7 in the number 47,820?', 'options': ['70', '700', '7,000 (Seven Thousand)', '70,000'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'In 47,820, the digit 7 is in the Thousands place, so its place value is 7 × 1,000 = 7,000.', 'study_insight': 'Position matters! The digit 4 in 47,820 is in the Ten Thousands place (value 40,000), while 7 is in the Thousands place (value 7,000).'}, {'question': 'What is the standard numeral for the expanded form: 60,000 + 3,000 + 500 + 80 + 2?', 'options': ['6,358', '63,582', '635,082', '60,358'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '6 Ten Thousands (60,000) + 3 Thousands (3,000) + 5 Hundreds (500) + 8 Tens (80) + 2 Ones (2) = 63,582.', 'study_insight': 'Combining place values reconstructs the standard numeral form of a number.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Writing Numbers', 'subtitle': 'Comma Placement and Reading Number Names in the Indian System', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">To read and write large numbers easily without getting confused by a long line of digits, we use <strong>commas</strong> to separate groups of place values called <strong>periods</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-3">\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Thousands Period (TTh, Th)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">The first two digits on the left represent thousands. Read together: e.g., <strong>48</strong> thousands in 48,215.</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800">\n                    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-400 block mb-1">Ones Period (H, T, O)</span>\n                    <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">The last three digits represent hundreds, tens, and ones: e.g., <strong>215</strong> (two hundred fifteen).</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-500/30 my-3 text-xs text-amber-200">\n                <strong>Indian Comma Rule:</strong> In the Indian system, the <em>first comma</em> is placed after 3 digits from the right (separating the Ones period: H, T, O). Subsequent commas are placed after every 2 digits! For a 5-digit number, we have exactly one comma: <strong>XX,XXX</strong>.\n            </div>\n            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Examples:</p>\n            <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 list-disc list-inside space-y-1">\n                <li><strong>24,560</strong> = Twenty-four thousand, five hundred sixty</li>\n                <li><strong>70,008</strong> = Seventy thousand, eight</li>\n                <li><strong>99,999</strong> = Ninety-nine thousand, nine hundred ninety-nine</li>\n            </ul>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Reading and writing large numbers becomes effortless when we use periods and commas according to the Indian system of numeration. In our Indian system, the digits are grouped into families called periods. The first three digits from the right—Ones, Tens, and Hundreds—form the Ones Period. To the left of the Hundreds place, we place our very first comma! The next two digits—Thousands and Ten Thousands—form the Thousands Period. This comma acts as a natural speaking pause. When reading a number, we read all the digits in the Thousands period together, say the word thousand, pause at the comma, and then read the remaining three digits in the Ones period. For example, look at the number four, eight, comma, two, one, five. The digits four and eight are in the Thousands period, making forty-eight thousand. After the comma, two, one, five is read as two hundred fifteen. So the complete number name is forty-eight thousand two hundred fifteen! If there are zeros, such as seven, zero, comma, zero, zero, eight, we read it simply as seventy thousand eight. Try entering different five-digit numbers in the comma formatter on your screen and listen to how neatly they are spoken and written!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Where is the comma correctly placed in the Indian system for the number 58240?', 'options': ['5,8240', '58,240', '582,40', '5824,0'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'In the Indian system, the first comma is placed after 3 digits from the right (after the hundreds digit): 58,240.', 'study_insight': "58,240 is read as 'Fifty-eight thousand, two hundred forty'."}, {'question': 'What is the correct number name for the numeral 80,050?', 'options': ['Eight thousand fifty', 'Eighty thousand, five hundred', 'Eighty thousand, fifty', 'Eight hundred thousand fifty'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': "80 is in the thousands period (eighty thousand), 0 in hundreds, and 50 in tens and ones. So it is read as 'Eighty thousand, fifty'.", 'study_insight': 'Zeroes act as place holders. Even if a place has zero, it preserves the positional value of the surrounding digits.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Ordering Numbers', 'subtitle': 'Comparing 5-Digit Numbers and Sorting in Increasing and Decreasing Order', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">When planning travel routes or checking train odometer readings, we often compare numbers to see which distance is greater or sort them from shortest to longest.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1.5">Rules for Comparing 5-Digit Numbers</span>\n                <ol class="text-xs text-slate-300 list-decimal list-inside space-y-1">\n                    <li><strong>Compare Highest Place First:</strong> Look at the Ten Thousands (TTh) digit. The number with the greater TTh digit is larger (e.g., <strong>6</strong>2,400 > <strong>4</strong>8,900).</li>\n                    <li><strong>If TTh digits are equal:</strong> Compare the Thousands (Th) digits (e.g., 5<strong>8</strong>,200 > 5<strong>3</strong>,400).</li>\n                    <li><strong>If Th digits are equal:</strong> Move to Hundreds, then Tens, and finally Ones.</li>\n                </ol>\n            </div>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-2 text-xs">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-sky-950/50 border border-sky-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-sky-300 block mb-1">Increasing (Ascending) Order</span>\n                    <p class="text-slate-300">From smallest to largest:<br>12,450 < 23,800 < 45,120 < 78,900</p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-emerald-300 block mb-1">Decreasing (Descending) Order</span>\n                    <p class="text-slate-300">From largest to smallest:<br>78,900 > 45,120 > 23,800 > 12,450</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'When planning travels and comparing journey logs, we often need to sort multi-digit numbers from smallest to greatest, which is ascending order, or from greatest to smallest, which is descending order. To compare two or more five-digit numbers, we follow a simple, golden rule: always compare the digits starting from the highest place value column, which is the Ten Thousands place! If the digits in the Ten Thousands place are different, the number with the greater digit is immediately the larger number. For instance, seventy-eight thousand two hundred is greater than thirty-four thousand five hundred because seven is greater than three. But what if the Ten Thousands digits are identical? Then we move one step to the right and compare the Thousands digits. If those are also equal, we compare the Hundreds digits, followed by Tens, and finally Ones. Look at the vehicle odometer logs on your screen. We have a delivery van at twelve thousand nine hundred kilometres, a car at thirty-four thousand five hundred, a bus at seventy-eight thousand two hundred, and a highway truck at ninety-five thousand four hundred. Use the sorting buttons to toggle between ascending and descending order, and notice how comparing the leftmost digits guarantees a correct ordering every single time!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which comparison symbol correctly fits in the blank: 45,820 ___ 45,790?', 'options': ['< (less than)', '> (greater than)', '= (equal to)', '≈ (approximately equal)'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'Both numbers have 4 in TTh and 5 in Th. In the Hundreds place, 8 is greater than 7. Therefore, 45,820 > 45,790.', 'study_insight': 'When the higher place value digits match, the next place value to the right breaks the tie.'}, {'question': 'Which of the following lists is sorted in strictly increasing (ascending) order?', 'options': ['34,500; 32,100; 45,000', '15,200; 18,400; 25,600; 42,100', '67,800; 54,300; 21,900; 10,000', '50,000; 40,000; 60,000'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': '15,200 < 18,400 < 25,600 < 42,100 is arranged from smallest to largest, which is ascending order.', 'study_insight': 'Ascending order goes up like climbing stairs, while descending order goes down like descending a mountain.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Digit Swap', 'subtitle': 'Digit Exchange Puzzles and Making Largest and Smallest Numbers', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">What happens when you swap two digits in a number? Because of our positional place value system, swapping digits drastically alters the number\'s total value!</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">The Power of Position</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Take the number <strong>25,480</strong>. If you swap the digits in the Ten Thousands (2) and Thousands (5) places, you get <strong>52,480</strong>.<br>The value increased by: 52,480 − 25,480 = <strong>27,000</strong>!</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 my-2 text-xs">\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-sky-950/50 border border-sky-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-sky-300 block mb-1">Forming the Largest Number</span>\n                    <p class="text-slate-300">Arrange given digits in <strong>descending order</strong> (highest digit in highest place):<br>Digits 3, 8, 1, 9, 5 → <strong>98,531</strong></p>\n                </div>\n                <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-500/30">\n                    <span class="font-bold text-emerald-300 block mb-1">Forming the Smallest Number</span>\n                    <p class="text-slate-300">Arrange digits in <strong>ascending order</strong> (if 0 is present, put it in second place):<br>Digits 4, 0, 7, 2, 9 → <strong>20,479</strong> (not 02,479!)</p>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Here is a captivating mathematical puzzle that reveals the true power of place values: what happens when you swap the positions of two digits in a large number? The face value of each digit remains the same, but because its place value changes, the entire number can increase or decrease dramatically! Let us investigate the odometer reading twenty-five thousand four hundred eighty. Here, the digit two is in the Ten Thousands place, worth twenty thousand, and the digit five is in the Thousands place, worth five thousand. Now, imagine swapping the two and the five! The new number becomes fifty-two thousand four hundred eighty. Notice what happened: the five is now in the Ten Thousands place, worth fifty thousand, while the two is in the Thousands place, worth two thousand. The new number is greater than the original by exactly twenty-seven thousand! Conversely, if you swap two digits where the smaller digit moves to a higher place, the total value drops. Test this on the interactive number shifter on your screen. Click the swap buttons, observe the place value transformations, and see how calculating the difference helps us solve real-world mathematical mystery puzzles!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Using the digits 7, 2, 9, 4, and 1 each exactly once, what is the largest 5-digit number you can form?', 'options': ['97,421', '97,241', '94,721', '12,479'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'To make the greatest number, arrange digits in descending order: 9 > 7 > 4 > 2 > 1 → 97,421.', 'study_insight': "Placing the largest available digit in the highest place value maximizes the number's total value."}, {'question': 'Using the digits 6, 0, 8, 3, and 5 each once, what is the smallest 5-digit number you can form?', 'options': ['03,568', '30,568', '35,068', '30,658'], 'correct': 1, 'explanation': 'We cannot put 0 in the first place because that would create a 4-digit number. The next smallest non-zero digit is 3, followed by 0, then 5, 6, 8 → 30,568.', 'study_insight': 'A leading zero does not count as a digit in whole numbers, so 03,568 is only a 4-digit number (3,568).'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Nearest Tens', 'subtitle': 'Rounding Off Numbers to the Nearest 10 Using Number Lines', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">In everyday travel, we often use approximate numbers. For example, if a bus has 48 passengers, we say it has "about 50 passengers". This practical mathematical skill is called <strong>Rounding Off</strong>.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-400 block mb-1">The Rabbit and Carrot Number Line</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Imagine a rabbit sitting on number <strong>43</strong> between two carrot patches at tens: <strong>40</strong> and <strong>50</strong>.<br>Distance to 40 = 3 hops. Distance to 50 = 7 hops.<br>The rabbit will hop to the closer food at <strong>40</strong>! So 43 rounded to nearest 10 is <strong>40</strong>.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-sky-950/40 border border-sky-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-sky-200">\n                <strong>The Halfway Rule:</strong> Look at the <em>Ones digit</em>:<br>\n                • If the Ones digit is <strong>0, 1, 2, 3, or 4</strong> (less than 5) → Round DOWN to the previous ten.<br>\n                • If the Ones digit is <strong>5, 6, 7, 8, or 9</strong> (5 or more) → Round UP to the next ten.<br>\n                <em>Example:</em> 45 is exactly halfway between 40 and 50. By mathematical convention, we round <strong>UP to 50</strong>.\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'In everyday travel, we frequently use approximate numbers instead of exact measurements. For example, if a bus has forty-eight passengers, we might say it has about fifty passengers. This practical skill is called rounding off! To round a number to the nearest ten, we imagine a number line marked in multiples of ten, such as forty and fifty. Picture a hungry rabbit standing at a position between forty and fifty. At forty, there is a bundle of carrots, and at fifty, there is another bundle. The rabbit naturally wants to hop to the nearest food! The decision depends entirely on the digit in the Ones column. If the Ones digit is one, two, three, or four, the number is closer to the lower ten, so we round down. For example, forty-three is closer to forty than to fifty, so forty-three rounds down to forty. But if the Ones digit is five, six, seven, eight, or nine, the number is closer to the higher ten, so we round up. When a number sits exactly in the middle with a five, such as forty-five, mathematical convention always rounds up to fifty! Drag the rabbit on your screen to different positions and observe how the Ones digit determines the nearest ten!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is 76 rounded to the nearest ten (10)?', 'options': ['70', '75', '80', '86'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': 'The Ones digit of 76 is 6 (which is 5 or more), so we round UP to the next ten: 80.', 'study_insight': 'On a number line, 76 is only 4 steps away from 80, but 6 steps away from 70. 80 is the nearest ten.'}, {'question': 'A train coach has 64 seats booked. Rounded to the nearest ten, how many seats are booked?', 'options': ['60', '65', '70', '100'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'The Ones digit is 4 (less than 5), so 64 is closer to 60 than to 70. Rounded to the nearest 10, it is 60.', 'study_insight': 'Numbers ending in 1, 2, 3, and 4 round down to the previous multiple of ten.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Nearest Hundreds', 'subtitle': 'Rounding to the Nearest 100 Using Benchmarks and Highway Milestones', 'concept_html': '\n            <p class="mb-3">When travellers drive down a highway, milestones often show distances rounded to hundreds of kilometres. To round a number to the <strong>nearest hundred</strong>, we find which multiple of 100 it is closest to.</p>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-950/60 border border-slate-800 my-3">\n                <span class="text-xs font-bold text-sky-400 block mb-1">Highway Milestone Benchmarks</span>\n                <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">Multiples of 100 are: 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 1,000, etc.<br>Between 300 and 400, the exact halfway mark is <strong>350</strong>.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 my-2 text-xs text-emerald-200">\n                <strong>Rule for Nearest 100:</strong> Look at the <em>Tens digit</em>:<br>\n                • If the last two digits are <strong>00 to 49</strong> (Tens digit < 5) → Round DOWN to the lower hundred.<br>\n                • If the last two digits are <strong>50 to 99</strong> (Tens digit ≥ 5) → Round UP to the higher hundred.<br>\n                <em>Examples:</em><br>\n                • <strong>328</strong> → Tens digit is 2 (< 5) → rounds to <strong>300</strong>.<br>\n                • <strong>372</strong> → Tens digit is 7 (≥ 5) → rounds to <strong>400</strong>.<br>\n                • <strong>350</strong> → Exactly halfway → rounds UP to <strong>400</strong>.\n            </div>\n        ', 'spoken_text': 'Now let us take our rounding skills to the highway and learn how to round off to the nearest hundred! Imagine driving along a long national highway where major milestone markers are placed every one hundred kilometres, such as at three hundred kilometres and four hundred kilometres. When we round to the nearest hundred, which digit holds the secret key? It is the digit in the Tens place! We examine the number formed by the last two digits: Tens and Ones. If the Tens digit is zero, one, two, three, or four—meaning the remaining value is between zero and forty-nine—the number is closer to the lower hundred, so we round down. For example, three hundred twenty-eight kilometres has a two in the Tens place. Because twenty-eight is less than fifty, three hundred twenty-eight rounds down to three hundred kilometres. However, if the Tens digit is five or more—meaning the remaining value is fifty to ninety-nine—we round up to the next hundred. For instance, three hundred fifty and three hundred seventy-two both round up to four hundred kilometres! Test this on the interactive highway car simulator by selecting different distances and watching the milestone marker snap to the nearest hundred!', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is 436 rounded to the nearest hundred (100)?', 'options': ['400', '430', '440', '500'], 'correct': 0, 'explanation': 'The Tens digit is 3 (less than 5), and 436 is closer to 400 (36 away) than to 500 (64 away). It rounds down to 400.', 'study_insight': 'Check the tens digit: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 round down; 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 round up.'}, {'question': 'A village bus travelled 750 km on a state tour. Rounded to the nearest hundred, what is the distance?', 'options': ['700 km', '750 km', '800 km', '1,000 km'], 'correct': 2, 'explanation': '750 is exactly halfway between 700 and 800. By mathematical rule, halfway numbers round UP to the higher hundred: 800 km.', 'study_insight': 'The halfway rule ensures consistency across mathematics and everyday trade.'}]}]
